@@ -4,6 +4,33 @@
 
 The crate keeps the public edge statically typed while the provider center is erased once at service construction. Calls are cheap to clone because operation payloads are shared, streams and sessions retain explicit ownership, errors are structured and secret-free, and receipts account for every attempt, recovery, usage dimension, and integer monetary unit. Provider identity and capability vocabulary come from `omp-catalog`; this crate does not infer policy from provider or model strings.
 
+## Response ownership and resource bounds
+
+Route admission covers the live response or realtime session, including its
+terminal bookkeeping. Finishing the HTTP handshake does not release a slot.
+Dropping readiness, a pending call, or a response releases its reservation;
+terminal streams release transport resources before admitting replacement work.
+Admission is process-local and its waiter count is bounded.
+
+Aggregate inference budgets reserve request allowance atomically and retain one
+accounting owner until the answer completes, fails, or is dropped. Final usage
+is charged once across retries and terminal receipts. Scopes with measured
+token, cost, or wall-time ceilings serialize outstanding requests because their
+final spend is not known at admission. Unreported usage cannot become free
+allowance. These are admission ceilings, not a promise that a single admitted
+request cannot exceed the remaining measured allowance.
+
+Compressed HTTP bodies enforce a cumulative decoded-output bound in the codec's
+output sink, before growing its output buffer. Both success and error bodies
+use this bound, and malformed or truncated encodings fail explicitly. The limit
+does not include the codec's separately bounded scratch/history windows or HTTP
+and frame buffers, and is not a whole-process memory ceiling.
+
+Run `just test-pkg omp-ai` for unit/integration tests and doctests, and
+`just clippy-pkg omp-ai` for the affected crate's lints. These tests require no
+live provider credentials. The response-lifetime and compressed-body
+regressions include local HTTP fixtures; they do not qualify live providers.
+
 ## Local TTS (Kokoro)
 
 The optional local TTS backend runs Kokoro-82M text-to-speech inference on [candle](https://github.com/huggingface/candle), with Metal acceleration on macOS. Its model pipeline combines an ALBERT text encoder, duration and prosody predictors with bidirectional LSTMs, and an iSTFTNet vocoder. It performs pure inference without downloading models or accessing the network: callers provide checkpoint weights and voice embeddings as candle tensors. The implementation is derived from Kyle Kelley's MIT-licensed `voice-kokoro` crate and includes modifications by Stencil Labs; see [NOTICE](NOTICE) and the retained [license](src/local/tts/kokoro/LICENSE).
